{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>To the heads of the tribes, etc.</b> One needs to know what is distinctive about this commandment that the verse specifies <b>the heads of the tribes</b>. And our Sages of blessed memory in Sifrei said: because in the passage of the trumpets He preceded the calling of the assembly to the princes, one might think that the assembly takes precedence over the princes — therefore Scripture says ״אֶל רָאשֵׁי״ etc., that they are the ones who come first. And elsewhere (Nedarim 78a, Bava Batra 120a) they said that it comes to teach the annulment of vows by an individual expert, and all this is by way of derash and asmakhtot. Further, one needs to know why it was necessary to say <b>the tribes of the children of</b> [Israel], for he should have said merely ״אֶל רָאשֵׁי מַטּוֹת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל.״ And it would appear that the intention was to speak in this order, lest you say that the commandment came only to the heads of the tribes of Israel alone — therefore he said ״אֶל רָאשֵׁי הַמַּטּוֹת לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל,״ as though he said to this one and to that one. And even though he did not say ״וְלִבְנֵי,״ many verses speak in this order, in the manner of his saying (Exodus 1:2) ״רְאוּבֵן שִׁמְעוֹן״ etc., as though he said ״וְשִׁמְעוֹן.״" "<b>However,</b> I saw a baraita (Bava Batra 120a) and this is its language: It was taught, Perhaps Moses did not say this passage except to the princes alone? It is stated here ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״ and it is stated regarding slaughter outside [the Sanctuary] (Leviticus 17) ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״ — just as there, etc., so too this was said to all of them. If so, there is no force to his saying ״לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ and ״לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל,״ since in the baraita they needed to prove it from a gezerah shavah, and our Sages of blessed memory already said (Keritot 5a), and this is their language: Never let a gezerah shavah be light in your eyes, etc." "Further, one needs to know the intent of his saying <b>saying</b>, for to whom would the children of Israel say it? And if the intention is that the heads of the tribes should say it to the children of Israel, he should have said ״לֵאמֹר לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל.״ Further, his saying <b>זֶה</b> הַדָּבָר — what does the word ״זֶה״ come to restrict?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the verse will be explained by prefacing the statement of our Sages of blessed memory, who said (Chagigah 10a), and this is their language: The annulment of vows flies in the air — end quote. And one should note why HaShem made of this commandment such an order, something He did not do in all the Torah, where every matter is written straightforwardly but this one flies in the air?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the reason is that HaShem did not want it written for the eyes of every person that vows and oaths have annulment available, for by this they would treat vows and oaths lightly. And therefore HaShem was wise and concealed the matter and delivered the annulment to the great ones of Israel, who would know and discern the distinctions of the laws within the annulment, but before all the masses they would be locked behind doors, to fulfill every vow and every oath, as He said ״לֹא יַחֵל״ etc., concluding ״כְּכָל הַיּוֹצֵא מִפִּיו״ etc. And this is the construal of the verse: <b>And Moses spoke to the heads of the tribes</b> — that is, to the great ones of Israel, [he gave] the commandment in its entirety; but <b>to the children of Israel, saying: This is the thing</b> — meaning that to the children of Israel collectively he would not say from the discourse spoken to the heads of the tribes except ״this is the thing,״ and not what flies in the air that was said orally to the heads of the tribes (Chagigah 10a). And with this all the fine points are resolved." "<b>Or</b> it may intend along this line: <b>to the heads of the tribes</b> the commandment comes, <b>and to the children of Israel, saying</b> — meaning, that one who vows or swears an oath among the children of Israel shall come and say it to the heads of the tribes, and they will declare to them what pertains to the prohibition of ״לֹא יַחֵל״ and what pertains to annulment, according to the laws that come under this judgment." "<b>And if</b> you say: according to all the interpretations, the children of Israel too are mentioned in this commandment, so why did our Rabbis of blessed memory need to derive the matter from what is explicitly stated? It may be answered that they did not need a gezerah shavah to derive this, but rather to establish that Moses spoke the words on his own to Israel — but for making the commandment known to Israel they did not need a gezerah shavah. And this is what they precisely asked: ״Perhaps Moses did not say this section except to the heads of the tribes,״ and they did not say ״Perhaps this section was not stated except to the heads of the tribes,״ which would have meant it was not stated at all." "<b>Further</b>, it may be said that without the gezerah shavah that informed us that this commandment applies to Israel, I could have interpreted His saying ״to the children of Israel״ as though He said ״of the children of Israel,״ and the matter would have been balanced [either way]. But once the gezerah shavah came and validated the commandment also for Israel, we settle the verse according to the [plain] manner stated. And one should not raise a difficulty against what we explained concerning ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״ — that it comes to restrict, so that one would not say to everyone that vows are annulled verbally — for indeed ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״ is needed for the gezerah shavah. For the gezerah shavah is derived from the word ״הַדָּבָר,״ whereas our interpretation is from the word ״זֶה״ alone — even had it said ״זֶה אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳.״" "<b>And by way of</b> remez, His saying <b>to the children of Israel, saying: this is the thing</b> may be explained based on what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 22:1), and this is their language: ״A person should always be careful with vows; King Yannai had a thousand cities...״ — see there that even though they fulfilled their oath they were punished. And the verse states here that there are vows and oaths that HaShem commanded them, and that is His saying <b>to the children of Israel, saying</b> — ״לֵאמֹר״ means nothing other than a warning against forbidden relations (Sanhedrin 56a), meaning a warning concerning the danger of forbidden relations — ״this is the thing that HaShem commanded: a man who vows a vow״ etc. And He was precise in saying <b>a man</b>, to allude to the overpowering force of the opposition of desire, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ruth Rabbah ch. 6) regarding the verse ״חַי ה׳ שִׁכְבִי״ etc., that he swore to his [evil] inclination, and likewise they said (Ḥagigah 10a) regarding the verse ״נִשְׁבַּעְתִּי וָאֲקַיֵּמָה לִשְׁמֹר״ etc., that it is a mitzvah to swear to one's [evil] inclination."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:19.101005673Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}